    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981529/assets/2ab9745cb9d157e566c101a6ca79514017903fa57eaf8725c846afd272c74f93.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?",
    "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
